The dental stems were also consonant stems, and had a stem ending in a dental consonant, ''th'', ''d'' ({{IPA|/ð/}}) or ''t'' ({{IPA|/d/}}). The final consonant itself was lost in the nominative and vocative singular. Unstressed word-final ''-th'' was generally converted to ''-d'' early on, so that the two types became indistinguishable in most forms.

