===Indeclinable nouns===
Some nouns were indeclinable. These include:
* Three native words meaning "choice": {{lang|sga|togu}}, {{lang|sga|rogu}}, and {{lang|sga|uccu}} 
* {{lang|sga|coí}} "weeping"
* {{lang|sga|dardaín}} "Thursday"
* Many names of Biblical figures (including {{lang|sga|Ísu}} "[[Jesus]]", {{lang|sga|Iohain}} "John", and {{lang|sga|Duaid}} "[[David]]")
* {{lang|sga|Patraic}} "[[Saint Patrick|Patrick]]" 
* Several mythical names beginning with the element ''da'', such as {{lang|sga|[[Da Derga]]}}.

==Adjectives==
Old Irish adjectives have four degrees of comparison, namely the positive, comparative, equative and superlative forms. In the positive degree, [[adjective]]s [[agreement (linguistics)|agree]] with nouns in [[grammatical case|case]], [[grammatical gender|gender]], and [[grammatical number|number]]. The other three degrees do not inflect for gender, number, or case.

Demonstrative adjectives have proximal, medial, and distal forms.

===Adjective inflection classes===
The adjectives in the positive degree are divided into four basic inflection types: o-ā-stems, io-iā-stems, i-stems, and u-stems. There are also two irregular consonant-stem adjectives, {{lang|sga|éola}} "knowledgeable" and {{lang|sga|té}} "hot".

