===Degrees of comparison===
In addition to the [[Markedness (linguistics)|unmarked]] positive degree, Old Irish adjectives have three other [[degrees of comparison]]. These are the equative, the comparative, and the superlative. All three of these are formed by suffixes; all three suffixes trigger vowel raising if applicable, in addition to causing syncope since they add extra syllables. A basic example of these suffixes is provided by {{lang|sga|soirb}} "easy", with its equative {{lang|sga|soirbidir}}, comparative {{lang|sga|soirbiu}}, and superlative {{lang|sga|soirbem}}.

Unlike in English, these other degrees of adjective are solely predicative and cannot directly modify other nouns attributively. If such an attributive use is desirable, a relative construction may be used instead:

{{interlinear|indent=3|lang=sga
|Cía thés hi loc bes ardu…
|although go.3SG.SBJV into place.ACC be.3SG.SBJV.REL higher
|"Although he may go into a higher place…"
}} <!-- Ml 23d23 -->

====Equative degree====
The equative degree is used for describing a noun having an adjective's qualities to the same degree as another noun. Equative adjectives are created by suffixing ''-ithir'' or ''-idir'' onto the adjective. Equative adjectives are followed by the accusative case of the noun being compared to. In the following example, {{lang|sga|áthithir}} "as sharp as" is the equative of {{lang|sga|áth}} "sharp".
{{interlinear|indent=3
|áthithir delc sciach
|sharp.{{gcl|EQ|equative degree}} thorn.ACC hawthorn.GEN
|"as sharp as a hawthorn thorn" (''[[Táin Bó Cúailgne]]'' recension I)
}}

====Comparative degree====
The comparative degree asserts that an adjective applies to a noun to a greater degree than to a different noun. It is formed by attaching the suffix {{lang|sga|-(i)u}} to the adjective's root. 

The other noun being compared to can appear standalone in the dative case. In the following example, {{lang|sga|ardu}} is the comparative of {{lang|sga|ard}} "high" and {{lang|sga|nim}} is the dative singular of {{lang|sga|nem}} "heaven". <!-- Thes. Pal. p. 235 -->
{{interlinear|indent=3|lang=sga
|Ní ardu ní nim.
|is.NEG higher anything.NOM heaven.DAT
|"Nothing is higher than Heaven."
}}

====Superlative degree====
The superlative degree denotes how the adjective applies to the greatest degree to a certain noun or pronoun than to any other relevant noun. It is formed with a suffix to the adjective's root. It takes on the form {{lang|sga|-em}} if the immediately preceding consonants can be palatalized and {{lang|sga|-am}} if they cannot. Several examples of superlatives are:

* {{lang|sga|caín}} "fine, fair" → {{lang|sga|caínem}} "finest, fairest"
* {{lang|sga|airgnae}} "well-known" → {{lang|sga|aurgnam}} "best-known"
* {{lang|sga|álaind}} "beautiful" → {{lang|sga|áildem}} "most beautiful"

====Irregularly formed degrees of comparison====
There exist some adjectives that form their degrees of comparison irregularly. Many of them involve [[suppletion]], where the other degrees are formed from a separate root to the positive degree. Others expose fossilized derivational suffixes that were attached to the positive degree in pre-Celtic times that were not also applied to the other degrees. 

Most of these irregular adjectives have a comparative ending in ''-a'', instead of the usual ''-(i)u'' comparative ending.

