Various irregularities in the phonological manifestations of the pronouns exist.
* The Class A pronoun ''-a'' (whether masculine or neuter) suppresses the final vowels of the preverbs it attaches to. On the other hand, it is itself suppressed by the negative particle ''ní'' "does not", leaving only mutational effects behind. For example, {{lang|sga|N'''a'''·chachnatar}} "they sang it (i.e. a song, the neuter noun {{lang|sga|céol}})" contains the infixed pronoun ''-a-'' in between the preverb ''no-'' and the next syllable, with ''-a-'' suppressing the preverb's vowel. But in {{lang|sga|Ní chachnatar}} "they did not sing it", the pronoun vanishes, leaving only lenition as the marker of its presence.
* Class B pronouns replace the final consonant of all their preverbs except ''etar-'' and ''for-''. They also irregularly merge ''in-'' with ''ad-'' when attached.
* Class C pronouns in the third-person singular masculine and neuter are further subdivided based on either phonological context or the particle preceding the verb.
** ''-d'' appears where the Class A pronoun would otherwise be used.
** ''-id'' appears where the Class B pronoun would otherwise be used.
** ''-did'' when the clause is preceded by ''i'' "into which" or ''co'' "so that".

There also existed a set of negative modal pronouns that substitute for the negative imperative particle {{lang|sga|ná}} and the negative relative or interrogative particle {{lang|sga|nád}} whenever a direct object pronoun was called for. These pronouns were built off a stem ''nach-'' or {{lang|sga|nách-}}.

